A suffix automaton stores all substrings in a compact graph. Each state keeps the number of times its represented substring ends in the original string. After building the automaton, those numbers are passed from longer states to their suffix-link states.

The original string's prefixes can then be followed from the start state. The stored value at each reached state is the answer for that prefix. This also counts overlaps, such as the three occurrences of `"aa"` in `"aaaa"`.

```go
func countPrefixOccurrences(str string) []int {
	type state struct {
		next map[byte]int
		link int
		length int
		occ int
	}

	states := make([]state, 0, 2*len(str)+1)
	newState := func() int {
		states = append(states, state{
			next: make(map[byte]int),
			link: -1,
		})
		return len(states) - 1
	}

	root := newState()
	last := root

	for i := 0; i < len(str); i++ {
		current := newState()
		states[current].length = states[last].length + 1
		states[current].occ = 1

		p := last
		for p != -1 {
			if _, exists := states[p].next[str[i]]; exists {
				break
			}
			states[p].next[str[i]] = current
			p = states[p].link
		}

		if p == -1 {
			states[current].link = root
		} else {
			q := states[p].next[str[i]]
			if states[p].length+1 == states[q].length {
				states[current].link = q
			} else {
				clone := newState()
				states[clone].length = states[p].length + 1
				states[clone].link = states[q].link
				for key, value := range states[q].next {
					states[clone].next[key] = value
				}

				for p != -1 {
					value, exists := states[p].next[str[i]]
					if !exists || value != q {
						break
					}
					states[p].next[str[i]] = clone
					p = states[p].link
				}

				states[q].link = clone
				states[current].link = clone
			}
		}
		last = current
	}

	maxLength := len(str)
	byLength := make([]int, maxLength+1)
	for _, item := range states {
		byLength[item.length]++
	}
	for i := 1; i <= maxLength; i++ {
		byLength[i] += byLength[i-1]
	}

	order := make([]int, len(states))
	for i := len(states) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
		length := states[i].length
		byLength[length]--
		order[byLength[length]] = i
	}

	for i := len(order) - 1; i > 0; i-- {
		current := order[i]
		parent := states[current].link
		if parent >= 0 {
			states[parent].occ += states[current].occ
		}
	}

	result := make([]int, 0, len(str))
	current := root
	for i := 0; i < len(str); i++ {
		current = states[current].next[str[i]]
		result = append(result, states[current].occ)
	}

	return result
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with the alphabet map operations treated as constant time. Space complexity: `O(n)` states and transitions. A one-character string is also handled normally, with an answer of 1.

The Z value at position `i` tells how many characters from the beginning of the string match starting at `i`. If that value is at least `k`, then the prefix of length `k` occurs there.

I store how many positions have each exact Z value, then build suffix totals. This changes those exact values into counts for every prefix length. Position zero is the original prefix occurrence, so one is added to every answer.

For `"aaaa"`, the Z values after the first character are `3, 2, 1`. The suffix totals give counts 4, 3, 2, and 1 for prefix lengths 1 through 4.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> countPrefixOccurences(string s) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());
        if (n == 0) {
            return {};
        }

        vector<int> z(n, 0);
        int left = 0;
        int right = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (i <= right) {
                z[i] = min(right - i + 1, z[i - left]);
            }

            while (i + z[i] < n && s[z[i]] == s[i + z[i]]) {
                z[i]++;
            }

            if (i + z[i] - 1 > right) {
                left = i;
                right = i + z[i] - 1;
            }
        }

        vector<int> exact(n + 1, 0);
        for (int value : z) {
            exact[value]++;
        }

        int running = 0;
        vector<int> result(n);
        for (int length = n; length >= 1; length--) {
            running += exact[length];
            result[length - 1] = running + 1;
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since the Z scan moves its matching window forward. Space complexity: `O(n)`. No input data is changed.

The prefix function from KMP gives the longest proper prefix that is also a suffix for every position. Those links let us count how often each prefix appears without checking every substring separately.

First, each position contributes to the prefix-function value ending there. Then counts are passed from longer prefixes to their smaller border prefixes. Finally, every prefix is added once for its own full occurrence.

A small dry run for `"abab"` gives counts `[2, 2, 1, 1]`: `"a"` appears twice, `"ab"` twice, and the longer prefixes once each.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> countPrefixOccurrences(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        int[] prefix = new int[n];

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            int j = prefix[i - 1];

            while (j > 0 && s.charAt(i) != s.charAt(j)) {
                j = prefix[j - 1];
            }

            if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) {
                j++;
            }
            prefix[i] = j;
        }

        int[] frequency = new int[n + 1];
        for (int value : prefix) {
            frequency[value]++;
        }

        for (int length = n; length > 0; length--) {
            int smallerBorder = prefix[length - 1];
            frequency[smallerBorder] += frequency[length];
        }

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int length = 1; length <= n; length++) {
            result.add(frequency[length] + 1);
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(n)`. The string is only read, not modified.

This version builds a trie from every suffix of the string. While inserting a suffix, each visited node gets one more count. A node reached after reading the first `k` characters therefore stores how many suffixes start with that prefix, which is exactly the number of appearances.

The method is easy to follow, but it uses much more memory than KMP or the Z function. It also handles repeated and overlapping matches without any special work.

An empty input produces an empty result. The original string is never changed.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    private class TrieNode {
        public Dictionary<char, TrieNode> Next = new Dictionary<char, TrieNode>();
        public int Count;
    }

    public IList<int> CountPrefixOccurences(string s) {
        List<int> counts = new List<int>();
        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();

        for (int start = 0; start < s.Length; start++) {
            TrieNode node = root;

            for (int index = start; index < s.Length; index++) {
                char current = s[index];

                if (!node.Next.ContainsKey(current)) {
                    node.Next[current] = new TrieNode();
                }

                node = node.Next[current];
                node.Count++;
            }
        }

        TrieNode prefixNode = root;
        for (int index = 0; index < s.Length; index++) {
            prefixNode = prefixNode.Next[s[index]];
            counts.Add(prefixNode.Count);
        }

        return counts;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`, because all suffixes are inserted. Space complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case when many trie nodes are different.

